 the 75 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (38). Reading's all-time high is 106\u00b0F, on July 22, 2011.","t_chg":"Reading's nights have warmed since 1985. Its days have warmed.","p_chg":"Across 127 complete years: Reading's average low shows no clear change: +0.00\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.09 to +0.09) that includes zero. Reading's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.20\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.14 to +0.26), about +2.7\u00b0F since 1889. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1985, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+1.17\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.86 to +1.48) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.74\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.35 to +1.13). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none. Highs rose in 8 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Reading has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Reading's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.36 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.09 to +0.62), about +5.5 inches since 1870. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 47.2 inches a year. The wettest year was 2018 (68.74 in); the driest, 1930 (27.68 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 8 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}